# Lakebase graph DB provisioning — don't hard-fail when no app is deployed yet

## Context

Debugging session started from a report that "Auto-Map isn't working" on the
Mapping page. That turned out to be user error (the edit lock had expired
from inactivity — clicking "Resume editing" fixed it) and a separate,
unrelated Knowledge Graph triplestore-sync failure (bad `lakebase_branch`
config, not a code bug). While chasing the KG sync failure, the user then
hit a real bug: the Settings → "Create graph DB" (Lakebase provisioning)
wizard aborted outright with "Could not resolve any app service principal;
nothing to grant. Check the app names." — even though no Databricks App has
been deployed yet (local-only dev, `ONTOBRICKS_APP_NAME` unset). The wizard
had already created the Lakebase instance/database/schema before failing,
but since the whole task raised, `on_success` never ran and the new graph DB
coordinates were never persisted into `graph_engine_config` — so the graph
DB was unusable even though it existed.

## Changes

1. `src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/provisioner.py` —
   `LakebaseGraphProvisioner._resolve_service_principals` no longer raises
   `ProvisionError` when zero app service principals resolve (no app
   deployed, or the configured app name doesn't exist yet). It now appends a
   warning and returns an empty mapping instead, consistent with how every
   other grant step in this module (and in `back/core/databricks/lakebase/grants.py`)
   already treats missing apps as best-effort/skippable rather than fatal.
   The downstream grant steps (`_step_grant_can_use`, `_step_grant_schema`)
   already no-op cleanly on an empty `sp_ids` dict, so the task now completes
   successfully and persists the instance/database/schema coordinates.
2. `src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/provisioner.py` —
   `LakebaseGraphProvisioner._validate_inputs` no longer requires
   `app_names` to be non-empty up front, for the same reason.

## Modified files

- `src/back/core/graphdb/lakebase/provisioner.py`

## Test result

- `uv run --frozen pytest -q tests/units/settings/test_lakebase_provision.py` — 12 passed.
- `uv run --frozen pytest -q -m "not scenario"` — 4688 passed, 347 skipped, 1 xfailed,
  14 failed, 14 errors. All 14 failures/errors are pre-existing and unrelated
  (Windows `cp1252` `UnicodeDecodeError` reading `app.yaml` in
  `test_app_yaml_template.py`, `test_deploy_app_rename_guard.py`,
  `test_deploy_instance_id.py`, `test_middleware_session.py`) — confirmed by
  re-running the same subset on `master` via `git stash`, which reproduces
  the identical failure set without this change.
